WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.070
In 1908, Allan Bennett, a British
man ordained as a monk in Burma,

2
00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:07.200
led a Buddhist mission to London.

3
00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:11.430
Today, every major school of
Buddhism is present in Britain.

4
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.500
Some British Buddhists have
family roots in Sri Lanka,

5
00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.200
Thailand or Burma.

6
00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:20.250
Others found Buddhism for
themselves, in a class or a book.

7
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.960
So is the Buddhism of a London meditation
class the same religion as the Buddhism

8
00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:26.310
of Bangkok?

9
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.760
In this mission, you'll trace
how Buddhism reached Britain,

10
00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:33.100
through scholars, early
converts and migration.

11
00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:36.100
Then you'll ask why Buddhism
became popular here,

12
00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.140
and how family roots and
personal choice shape practice.

13
00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:45.950
Your mission: decide whether a London class
and a Bangkok temple share one Buddhism.
